      "worked": "Put $A=a\\cos\\alpha-b\\sin\\beta$ and $B=a\\sin\\alpha+b\\cos\\beta$. By the compound-angle formulas,\n$$\\begin{align*}a\\sin(\\theta+\\alpha)+b\\cos(\\theta+\\beta)&=a\\sin\\theta\\cos\\alpha+a\\cos\\theta\\sin\\alpha+b\\cos\\theta\\cos\\beta-b\\sin\\theta\\sin\\beta\\\\a\\sin(\\theta+\\alpha)+b\\cos(\\theta+\\beta)&=A\\sin\\theta+B\\cos\\theta.\\end{align*}$$\nChoose $c=\\sqrt{A^2+B^2}$. If $c=0$, then $A=B=0$, so both sides are zero with $\\phi=0$.\n\nIf $c>0$, the point $(\\frac{A}{c},\\frac{B}{c})$ lies on the unit circle. Hence there is a real angle $\\phi$ such that $\\cos\\phi=\\frac{A}{c}$ and $\\sin\\phi=\\frac{B}{c}$. Therefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}a\\sin(\\theta+\\alpha)+b\\cos(\\theta+\\beta)&=c\\cos\\phi\\sin\\theta+c\\sin\\phi\\cos\\theta\\\\a\\sin(\\theta+\\alpha)+b\\cos(\\theta+\\beta)&=c\\sin(\\theta+\\phi).\\end{align*}$$\nThis holds for every $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R$. The amplitude can also be written as\n$$\\begin{align*}c^2&=(a\\cos\\alpha-b\\sin\\beta)^2+(a\\sin\\alpha+b\\cos\\beta)^2\\\\c^2&=a^2+b^2+2ab(\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\beta-\\cos\\alpha\\sin\\beta)\\\\c^2&=a^2+b^2+2ab\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta).\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
